  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how soil forms and functions under different vegetation. On the left, a time series shows the gradual development of soil from unweathered bedrock into a deeper, well-developed profile. As time progresses, rock breaks down into smaller fragments, a thicker brown soil layer accumulates, and plant cover shifts from low shrubs to larger trees. This highlights soil as a product of long-term weathering plus biological activity.\n\nThe two right panels compare belowground structure in a natural tree-based system versus a crop field. Under the tree, roots extend deeply and densely through the soil, and the magnified view suggests active soil life, including earthworms and channels or pores around roots. The soil appears darker and more structured near the surface, indicating richer organic matter and biological mixing. In contrast, beneath the row crop, roots are shallower and more concentrated near the surface. The magnified view suggests a thinner biologically active top layer, compacted or layered subsurface zones, and less extensive pore networks, with fewer visible organisms near the rooting zone. Overall, the figure emphasizes that soil develops over time and that perennial woody vegetation tends to support deeper rooting and more biologically complex soil than annual cropping.",
